# Shadow SITL boot params - applied automatically via sim_vehicle.py's
# --add-param-file, so ArduCopter arms cleanly on every fresh SITL boot
# without needing a human at the MAVProxy console.
#
# FRAME_CLASS/FRAME_TYPE were previously set by hand, once per session,
# typed directly into the MAVProxy console after hitting
# "PreArm: Motors: Check frame class and type" during Gate 1/2 tuning
# (see areas/shadow-ardupilot-migration-roadmap.md, "PreArm: Motors --
# Check frame class and type - RESOLVED"). Baking the fix in here is
# what actually makes an unattended/scripted boot (Zero Command's
# "Start Simulation" button) arm correctly with no one typing at a
# console - the manual fix only ever lived in that one MAVProxy
# session's live parameter state, never persisted anywhere until now.
FRAME_CLASS 1
FRAME_TYPE  1

# Oct 24, 2026 - the actual root cause of the entire "local_position
# never delivers data" saga (search-and-deliver mission debugging log,
# areas/search-and-deliver-mission.md, Run 5/6). Confirmed live:
# MAV1_POSITION and MAV1_EXTRA1 were both 0 on every single boot -
# ArduCopter was never sending LOCAL_POSITION_NED or ATTITUDE/IMU data
# to MAVROS's link at all, no matter how long anything waited. This is
# the modern (V4.8.0-dev) replacement for the old SRx_* stream-rate
# scheme - one bank per connected GCS link (MAV1/MAV2/MAV3) rather than
# per serial port. It was never set anywhere because --no-mavproxy
# (run_full_sim.sh) means nothing is at a console to request these
# streams the way MAVProxy normally does automatically on connect, and
# MAVROS itself never proactively requests them either - it only
# listens for whatever arrives. /mavros/state (heartbeat-driven) always
# worked fine throughout this whole debugging arc for exactly that
# reason: HEARTBEAT is unconditional, these are not. Confirmed live via
# `ros2 service call /mavros/param/set mavros_msgs/srv/ParamSetV2` that
# setting these to 10 makes the FCU accept and keep the value - baking
# them in here so every fresh boot has them from the start, the same
# reason FRAME_CLASS/FRAME_TYPE are baked in above.
MAV1_POSITION 10
MAV1_EXTRA1   10
MAV1_EXTRA2   4
MAV1_EXT_STAT 2
MAV1_RAW_SENS 2

# Nov 7, 2026 - same bug class as MAV1_POSITION/MAV1_EXTRA1/MAV1_RC_CHAN
# above, found while trying to verify whether tonight's parameter changes
# (RC3_MIN/MAX/TRIM/REVERSED, MOT_HOVER_LEARN, RC_OVERRIDE_TIME) were ever
# actually landing on the FC - `ros2 service call /mavros/param/get ...`
# just hangs forever on "waiting for service to become available," live,
# not the old 5s-timeout flakiness this script's own param_get_client has
# logged all session. MAVROS's param plugin needs ArduCopter to actually
# send PARAM_VALUE messages to do anything, and MAV1_PARAMS - the stream-
# rate bank for exactly that message - was never set anywhere in this
# file, same unset-and-defaulting-to-off shape as every other missing
# MAV1_* gap tonight. Not independently confirmed from a primary source
# (same ardupilot.org/GitHub provenance block hit everywhere else in this
# file) - inferred from the naming pattern this file already establishes
# and from the fact that the already-known "flaky /mavros/param/get"
# complaint has been on record in this project for a while with no root
# cause ever pinned on it. Cheap to test: no colcon build needed, just a
# fresh "Start Simulation," and the test is unambiguous either way - if
# `ros2 service call /mavros/param/get ...` returns an actual value after
# this, the gate was here; if it still hangs, this guess was wrong too and
# the param service is broken for some other reason entirely.
MAV1_PARAMS   10

# Nov 6, 2026 - same bug class as MAV1_POSITION/MAV1_EXTRA1 above, found
# while trying to get real per-rotor PWM telemetry out of autotune_hover.py
# (see areas/pid-autotune-dashboard.md, Session 6). `ros2 topic info
# /mavros/rc/out --verbose` showed an advertised publisher but `ros2 topic
# hz /mavros/rc/out` produced NOTHING for 10+ seconds - MAVROS has the
# publisher object but ArduPilot was never actually sending the underlying
# SERVO_OUTPUT_RAW MAVLink message it depends on. MAV1_RC_CHAN (the
# RC/servo-channel stream bank) was simply never set anywhere in this file,
# same root shape as the already-fixed MAV1_POSITION/MAV1_EXTRA1 gap.
#
# NOT independently confirmed from a primary source - ardupilot.org and
# every GitHub source fetch for GCS_Mavlink.cpp/GCS_Common.cpp were both
# blocked for this session (same blocked-provenance issue already hit
# elsewhere in this project). This is inferred from the naming pattern the
# rest of this file already establishes (MAV1_POSITION->position messages,
# MAV1_EXTRA1->attitude, etc.) plus general ArduPilot stream-group
# knowledge, not verified against source. Cheap to test either way: this
# is a plain SITL param, no colcon build needed, just a fresh "Start
# Simulation" - if `ros2 topic hz /mavros/rc/out` still shows nothing after
# this, the real gating parameter is something else and this guess was
# wrong.
MAV1_RC_CHAN  10

# Oct 26, 2026 (even later) - confirmed live via a grep of this file
# that BATT_MONITOR was never set, which is the actual root cause of
# the Training Log's battery chart always reading "not enough telemetry
# to chart": with BATT_MONITOR disabled (the ArduCopter default),
# ArduCopter never sends BATTERY_STATUS over MAVLink at all, so
# telemetry_bridge.py's _battery_cb() callback simply never fires - not
# a bug in that callback, there was just never anything to receive.
# BATT_MONITOR 4 (Analog Voltage and Current) is SITL's own standard
# simulated-battery monitor - it doesn't need real ADC hardware, SITL's
# internal battery model drives it directly. BATT_CAPACITY matches the
# real FCONEGY 1500mAh 3S pack from the component library (see the
# Digital Twin's own battery spec card), so the percentage-remaining
# SITL reports tracks a capacity that's actually real, not a made-up
# default. Same "baked into the boot param file so Start Simulation
# needs no manual console step" reasoning as FRAME_CLASS/MAV1_* above -
# takes effect on the next fresh "Start Simulation", no colcon build
# needed, this file is read straight from source by run_full_sim.sh.
BATT_MONITOR  4
BATT_CAPACITY 1500

# Nov 6, 2026 (later, same night) - the MAV1_RC_CHAN fix above finally
# produced real per-rotor PWM telemetry out of autotune_hover.py's
# hover-throttle search, and it shows something new and much more
# concrete than the attitude-mixer-saturation guess it replaces (see
# areas/pid-autotune-dashboard.md, Session 6): raising the RC3 override
# from 1650 to 1800 PWM made the REAL motor output on all four rotors go
# DOWN, not up - 1151 -> 1102 -> 1100 (the servo floor), identically
# across all four motors (no asymmetry, no tilt - roll/pitch stayed flat
# throughout, which rules the mixer-saturation idea out directly). That
# is backward, and it's exactly what a reversed or swapped RC3 channel
# calibration would produce: ArduPilot normalizes the raw RC3 PWM through
# its own RC3_MIN/RC3_MAX/RC3_TRIM/RC3_REVERSED before that value ever
# reaches the motor mixer - if that calibration is flipped, a higher raw
# override maps to a LOWER internal throttle command, on every motor
# identically, which is exactly this data.
#
# NOT independently confirmed by reading the live parameter first -
# /mavros/param/get has been flaky all session (same issue documented
# since the Sep 21 PX4-era "New features wanted next" section) and I did
# not want to block this fix on it. These are the known-correct values
# (RC3_MIN/RC3_MAX matching the 1100/1900 servo range already confirmed
# in ardu_ws/src/ardupilot_gazebo/models/shadow_with_ardupilot/model.sdf,
# RC3_TRIM at the PWM midpoint, RC3_REVERSED explicitly off) - safe to set
# explicitly either way, since this can't break a channel that was
# already correct, only fix one that wasn't. Same "bake the real fix into
# the boot file, no manual console step needed" reasoning as FRAME_CLASS/
# MAV1_* above - takes effect on the next fresh "Start Simulation", no
# colcon build needed.
RC3_MIN      1100
RC3_MAX      1900
RC3_TRIM     1500
RC3_REVERSED 0

# Nov 6, 2026 (later still, same night) - new hypothesis for the throttle-decay
# anomaly, now that a diagnostic hold (streaming ONE fixed PWM for 2s before
# the adaptive search starts - see autotune_hover.py's
# _find_hover_throttle_in_stabilize() docstring) has proven two things: (1)
# the decay is time-dependent, not command-dependent - real rotor PWM still
# fell from ~1150 to 1100 while the commanded PWM never changed at all - and
# (2) at 20Hz logging resolution the fall is a smooth, gradual RAMP (flat
# until ~t=0.6s, then sliding down tick by tick to the floor by ~t=1.0s), not
# a sudden cliff. A sudden cliff would point at a message/mode timeout
# snapping to some fallback value; a smooth ramp instead fits a mechanism that
# keeps CONTINUOUSLY adjusting its own output while the vehicle sinks.
# MOT_HOVER_LEARN is exactly that kind of mechanism: left at its default, it
# continuously updates ArduCopter's learned hover-throttle estimate during
# flight. If Shadow has never had a stable hover to learn a correct estimate
# from, and is actively sinking because of this very bug, that creates a
# self-reinforcing loop - learned estimate falls -> commanded output derived
# from it falls further -> sink rate increases -> estimate falls further -
# which would produce precisely the gradual ramp just measured, with no need
# for any external command change.
#
# NOT independently confirmed from a primary source - same ardupilot.org/
# GitHub provenance block hit everywhere else in this file. This is a
# hypothesis test, not a confirmed fix: disabling hover-learning is also
# standard safe practice during tuning work regardless of whether this
# specific theory turns out to be right, so there is no downside to setting
# it now. If the next run's diagnostic-hold ramp looks identical with this
# set to 0, the self-reinforcing-hover-learning theory is wrong and something
# else is still falling on its own.
MOT_HOVER_LEARN 0

# Nov 7, 2026 - the first lead tonight backed by an actual ArduPilot doc
# fetch rather than pattern-matching or a blind guess. "Searching for the
# real hover throttle" run's diagnostic hold has now been checked against
# /mavros/rc/in (RC_CHANNELS - what ArduPilot itself reports as RC input):
# it read a flat 1000 the ENTIRE hold, never once showing the 1650 this
# script was continuously streaming as an override. ardupilot.org/dev/
# docs/mavlink-rcinput.html (fetched directly, not from memory) confirms:
# "If MAVLink based RC input is sent from the GCS this will generally
# override the regular RC input," but also "If the MAVLink RC input
# stops, ArduPilot falls back to regular RC input within a few seconds...
# configurable using the RC_OVERRIDE_TIME parameter." That page did NOT
# state ArduCopter's default RC_OVERRIDE_TIME value, and did NOT confirm
# whether RC_CHANNELS reports the override value or the raw/underlying
# value regardless - so the rc_in_thr=1000 reading doesn't, by itself,
# prove the override truly never arrived; it may just be the wrong
# telemetry to answer that (RC_CHANNELS could legitimately report the
# "raw" value underneath an active override, not the override itself -
# unconfirmed either way). What DOES fit disturbingly well: every single
# diagnostic-hold run tonight, regardless of what else changed, shows the
# same real-motor-output decay shape - flat for roughly the first 0.6-0.7s,
# then a smooth ramp down to the hard floor by roughly t=1.0-1.1s. That is
# exactly the shape an RC_OVERRIDE_TIME expiry would produce if this
# script's override stream isn't actually being received continuously
# by ArduPilot (even on the RELIABLE QoS fixed earlier - reliable delivery
# isn't the same guarantee as "delivered often enough, with no gaps, to
# never hit a short timeout"). Setting this generously high (10s, comfortably
# longer than the whole diagnostic hold) is a cheap, reversible, easy to
# interpret test: if the decay disappears or is pushed out well past 1
# second, the override genuinely was expiring and this is the real root
# cause. If the exact same ~0.6-1.1s ramp happens anyway, this parameter
# was not the gating one and the override-timeout theory is wrong too.
RC_OVERRIDE_TIME 10

# Nov 7, 2026 (after EXTENDED_SYS_STATE finally came online via explicit
# MAV_CMD_SET_MESSAGE_INTERVAL - see autotune_hover.py's
# _request_message_interval()) - landed_state read IN_AIR for the ENTIRE
# diagnostic hold on the next real run, abort line included. That's a
# clean, conclusive result, not another dead instrument: the land-
# detector hypothesis is DISPROVEN with real telemetry, same as mixer-
# saturation, RC3 calibration, MOT_HOVER_LEARN, and RC_OVERRIDE_TIME
# before it.
#
# Next angle, and a genuinely different ArduCopter subsystem from land
# detection: AP_Motors' own spool state machine (GROUND_IDLE ->
# SPOOLING_UP -> THROTTLE_UNLIMITED -> SPOOLING_DOWN), which runs
# independently of whether the FC believes it has landed. If switching
# from GUIDED into STABILIZE is forcing a spool-state reset regardless
# of landed_state, that would still explain a uniform, all-four-motors,
# command-independent collapse toward the output floor with no tilt -
# the land-detector hypothesis and this one share almost the same
# predicted symptom, which is exactly why ruling out one doesn't rule
# out the other.
#
# NOT independently confirmed from a primary source (same provenance
# block hit everywhere else in this file) - MOT_SPOOL_TIME is believed
# to be the real parameter name from general ArduCopter knowledge, not
# verified against current source. Set deliberately low here (not off -
# I'm not aware of a way to disable spooling entirely) so the test has a
# sharper signature than the last four "did the exact same thing happen
# or not" guesses: if this is the real gating timer, the ~0.6s-flat-
# then-ramp-to-floor shape should visibly SPEED UP, not just disappear
# or stay identical - watch the TIMING on the next diagnostic hold, not
# just whether it still happens.
#
# Nov 7, 2026 (same night, later) - the prediction above came true, cleanly:
# with MOT_SPOOL_TIME at 0.1, the next diagnostic hold's real rotor PWM was
# still flat ~1150 through t=0.1s (same as every prior run), then was
# ALREADY pinned at the 1100 floor by t=0.2s, and stayed dead flat at 1100
# for the remaining second of the hold. Every prior run (MOT_SPOOL_TIME at
# whatever its default was) took until roughly t=0.9-1.1s to bottom out.
# Shrinking this parameter ~5-10x compressed the collapse by roughly the
# same factor - the first time tonight a parameter change produced a
# PROPORTIONAL timing shift instead of a flat "no effect" or "same thing
# happened anyway," which is a much harder result to get by accident.
#
# Confirming test, the other direction: with MOT_SPOOL_TIME set to 10,
# the vehicle never even reached 6m in GUIDED within the 20s climb
# timeout - no diagnostic hold data at all that run. This fetched
# straight from ArduPilot's own source this time (libraries/AP_Motors/
# AP_MotorsMulticopter.cpp, GitHub, Nov 7 2026), not general knowledge:
# MOT_SPOOL_TIME ("SPOOL_TIME", @Range 0.05-2, no upper clamp in code,
# only a lower one) sets _spool_up_time, and the SAME variable gates the
# SPOOLING_UP throttle-ceiling ramp used right after every arm+takeoff
# in EVERY flight mode, not just the STABILIZE-only spool-down ramp
# tested above (spool_step = _dt_s / _spool_up_time, both directions).
# At 10 seconds that ramp isn't done until most of the 20s climb window
# has already elapsed, so this wasn't a new unrelated bug - it's the
# same real, now source-confirmed lever, just hit from the other side.
# Reverting to ArduCopter's own compiled default (removing the override
# entirely) rather than guessing a "safe" middle value, now that a much
# more likely root cause has been found below - see SYSID_MYGCS.

# Nov 7, 2026 - the actual root cause, found by reading ArduPilot's own
# MAVLink override handler and MAVROS's own UAS source tonight (GitHub,
# both primary source, not guesses):
#   libraries/GCS_MAVLink/GCS_Common.cpp, handle_rc_channels_override():
#     "if (!gcs().sysid_is_gcs(msg.sysid)) { return; }
#      // Only accept control from our gcs"
#   libraries/GCS_MAVLink/GCS.cpp: AP_GROUPINFO("_GCS_SYSID", ..., 255)
#     - the GCS-sysid-allowlist param defaults to 255.
#   mavros/src/lib/mavros_uas.cpp, UAS::UAS(): "source_system(target_system_)"
#     - MAVROS sends every message it transmits to the FC, override
#       included, using the SAME sysid as its own tgt_system arg, which
#       apm.launch defaults to 1 (matching ArduCopter's own default
#       SYSID_THISMAV=1) - MAVROS never introduces a separate "GCS" id
#       of its own unless told to.
# Put together: every single RC_CHANNELS_OVERRIDE message this script
# has sent all night, no matter how correct the ROS2/MAVROS side of it
# was (and we DID find and fix a real bug there too - the QoS mismatch
# a few runs back), has been arriving at the FC as sysid=1 and getting
# silently discarded at this exact one-line gate before it ever reaches
# RC_Channels::set_override() - because 1 != 255. This fits every
# symptom tonight at once: constant commanded PWM having zero effect,
# /mavros/rc/in's rc_in_thr sitting at 1000 no matter what we send (it's
# reporting ArduPilot's own fallback raw input, because the override
# never registers), and RC_OVERRIDE_TIME changing nothing (there was
# never an active override for it to time out). Fix: make the FC accept
# sysid 1 as a valid GCS, matching what MAVROS actually sends, instead
# of trying to make MAVROS pretend to be sysid 255.
#
# Nov 7, 2026 (same night, minutes later) - that run came back IDENTICAL:
# same ~1s collapse, same abort altitude, rc_in_thr still pinned at 1000.
# The mechanism above is still right, but I wrote it down wrong. Checked
# ArduPilot's own Tools/scripts/convert_params_4_7.py (GitHub, primary
# source): `cps.Rename('SYSID_MYGCS', 'MAV_GCS_SYSID')` - SYSID_MYGCS is
# the OLD name. This firmware's current parameter table uses the new
# name below; the rename table only migrates a value already stored
# under the old name, it doesn't make a fresh --defaults-file entry
# under the old name do anything. My line almost certainly loaded as an
# unrecognized parameter and was silently ignored - same bug, wrong key.
MAV_GCS_SYSID 1

# Nov 8, 2026 - navigation speed and RTL behaviour. ArduPilot 4.7+ names
# and units (metres, m/s); the old WPNAV_SPEED / RTL_ALT (cm) names were
# renamed in 4.7. Applied on every fresh SITL boot via --add-param-file.
#
# WP_SPD 3 m/s (a deliberate cap): Shadow hovers at ~70% throttle,
# so hard acceleration costs height - a 12 m go_to leg at 1 m altitude
# ended on the ground. Gentler speed + acceleration = shallower tilt.
WP_SPD 3.0
WP_ACC 1.5
# RTL: return at 3 m (inside shadow/limits.py's 6 m ceiling; the old
# 15 m default would trip limits() mid-return), land all the way at home.
RTL_ALT_M 3.0
RTL_ALT_FINAL_M 0

# Nov 9, 2026 - LiDAR (lidar_2d in model.sdf, RPLIDAR A2M12 on the real
# build). lidar_bridge.py sends 72 x 5 deg OBSTACLE_DISTANCE messages
# through MAVROS's obstacle plugin. Names checked against ArduPilot
# master source (metres throughout).
# PRX1_TYPE 2 = MAVLink. If the bridge is not running, ArduPilot blocks
# arming with "PRX1: No Data" - set PRX1_TYPE 0 to fly without LiDAR.
PRX1_TYPE 2
PRX1_MIN 0.3
PRX1_MAX 12
# Nov 9 (later) - ROLLED BACK. With BendyRuler on, every go_to and
# follow_path timed out (go_to had passed 12 in a row before), Shadow
# swung off course, and RTL failed limits() instead of landing home.
# Avoidance stays OFF for normal flying until the dedicated avoid test
# proves it. LiDAR data still reaches ArduPilot (PRX1 above) and the
# radar; it just doesn't steer the drone.
# AVOID_ENABLE 1 = fence only (proximity bit off), same behaviour as
# before the LiDAR existed.
AVOID_ENABLE 1
AVOID_MARGIN 1.0
OA_TYPE 0
OA_MARGIN_MAX 1.5
GUID_OPTIONS 0
